        <div id="lb-last-wrap" hidden><label for="lb-last">Which number did you try?</label><select id="lb-last" aria-describedby="lb-route-error"><option value="">Choose the number type</option><option value="mobile">Mobile</option><option value="direct">Work direct or extension</option><option value="company">Company or local branch</option></select></div>
        <p class="lp-small lb-tool-help">The answer updates as you choose. A preferred number or agreed callback takes priority.</p><p id="lb-route-error" class="lp-error" role="alert"></p>
      </form>
      <aside class="lb-result" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"><p class="lp-kicker">CallTeam's starting recommendation</p><div id="lb-result-copy"><h3 id="lb-result-title">Try the mobile first.</h3><p id="lb-result-why">This buyer usually works away from a desk.</p><div class="lb-next" id="lb-next"><strong>Next step</strong><p id="lb-result-next">No mobile? Try their work direct line. If you only have the company number, ask for them by name.</p></div></div><button class="lb-copy" type="button" data-copy="lb-result-copy" data-status="lb-route-copy-status">Copy this next step</button><span id="lb-route-copy-status" class="lb-copy-status" role="status"></span></aside>
    </div>
    <noscript><div class="lp-note"><strong>Your next-call rules</strong><p>Known buyer away from a desk: start with mobile. Known office-based buyer: start with work direct. Buyer unknown: use the company route to identify them. After voicemail, plan a later attempt or another suitable route. Answer an AI screen clearly. Remove a confirmed wrong number. Honour callbacks and stop requests.</p></div></noscript>
    <p class="lp-small">These are starting recommendations, not measured rankings. Keep the same account owner across routes so two reps do not call the same business without knowing it.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="lp-section" id="screening"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">03 / When a person does not answer</p><h2>Voicemail, AI screening<br>and reception need different responses.</h2><p>Listen before deciding what happened. An automated answer is not a conversation with your buyer.</p></div>
    <div class="lb-response-grid">
      <article class="lb-response"><p class="lp-kicker">AI call screening</p><h3>Give a clear reason.</h3><p>Apple and Google can ask for your name and reason before the recipient chooses whether to answer. Keep it short, answer the prompt and wait. A useful explanation can be passed on; it does not guarantee acceptance.</p><blockquote id="lb-ai-script">Alex at CallTeam. I'm calling to see whether [Name] needs help booking sales meetings.</blockquote><button class="lb-copy lb-js-only" type="button" data-copy="lb-ai-script" data-status="lb-ai-status" hidden>Copy screening response</button><span id="lb-ai-status" class="lb-copy-status" role="status"></span><p class="lb-action"><strong>Log:</strong> screened, plus what happened next. If the person declines or asks you to stop, record that separately.</p></article>
      <article class="lb-response"><p class="lp-kicker">Voicemail</p><h3>Leave one useful reason.</h3><p>If the mailbox belongs to your intended contact, give your name, the business reason and a callback number. Do not read the full pitch into the greeting. An unanswered call does not establish that the data is wrong.</p><blockquote id="lb-voicemail-script">Hi [Name], Alex at CallTeam. We help B2B teams book sales meetings through human calling. If that's relevant, you can reach me at [your callback number].</blockquote><button class="lb-copy lb-js-only" type="button" data-copy="lb-voicemail-script" data-status="lb-voicemail-status" hidden>Copy voicemail example</button><span id="lb-voicemail-status" class="lb-copy-status" role="status"></span><p class="lb-action"><strong>Log:</strong> voicemail or no answer. Set a later attempt under your campaign's contact rules.</p></article>
      <article class="lb-response"><p class="lp-kicker">Reception or phone menu</p><h3>Find the responsible person.</h3><p>At a company number, first confirm the right business or branch. Ask about the role that owns the problem. Use any name or extension you learn to make the next call more specific.</p><blockquote id="lb-reception-script">Hi, Alex at CallTeam. Who looks after outbound sales and appointment setting for your team?</blockquote><button class="lb-copy lb-js-only" type="button" data-copy="lb-reception-script" data-status="lb-reception-status" hidden>Copy reception example</button><span id="lb-reception-status" class="lb-copy-status" role="status"></span><p class="lb-action"><strong>Log:</strong> the name, role, transfer route and any agreed next step. Keep shared numbers attached to the account.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="lp-small">Adapt these examples to the actual service you sell. Replace every bracket before calling. A prompt saying the recipient will call back is not an appointment or permission to keep calling.</p>
    <div class="lb-screen-note"><h3>A call can go to voicemail without ringing.</h3><p>Some phone settings silence unknown callers or filter suspected spam. Availability and behaviour depend on the device, region and settings. You cannot diagnose the cause from a single unanswered call.</p><p><strong>Our recommendation:</strong> review a few call outcomes before changing the list or dialler. If your system treats a screening assistant as voicemail, check how that setting handles a real screening prompt. Keep buyer conversations separate from automated answers.</p><p class="lp-sources"><strong>Official sources checked {{ guide.reviewed }}:</strong> <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-ca/111106" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apple: managing unknown callers ↗</a> · <a href="https://support.google.com/phoneapp/answer/9118387?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google: Call Screen ↗</a> · <a href="https://support.google.com/phoneapp/answer/3459196?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google: caller ID and spam protection ↗</a></p></div>
    <div class="lb-inline-links"><a href="/articles/b2b-cold-call-voicemail-scripts-that-create-a-reason-to-call-back/">More B2B voicemail examples →</a><a href="/articles/how-to-get-past-b2b-gatekeeper-without-sounding-deceptive/">Handling gatekeepers →</a><a href="/articles/how-to-answer-how-did-you-get-my-number-b2b-cold-call/">When they ask where you got the number →</a></div>
    <div class="gr-resources" aria-labelledby="lb-script-resources"><h3 id="lb-script-resources">Be ready when the buyer answers.</h3><p>A screening response gets you through the prompt. Prepare the opening and questions for the conversation that follows.</p>{% include "components/free-calling-resources.njk" %}</div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="lp-section lp-dark" id="buying-decision"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">04 / A decision from the calling floor</p><h2>The mobile pass is finished.<br>Is the list actually finished?</h2><p>Here is a worked example based on the way we organise calling lists.</p></div>
    <div class="lb-case"><div><p>Your CRM shows no uncalled mobiles. There are still suitable companies with unworked branch numbers. There are also unanswered contacts and callbacks due later.</p><p><strong>Our move:</strong> separate those queues before buying again. Work the branch numbers where the account fit is strong. Keep agreed callbacks on their own schedule. Research missing routes at the accounts worth pursuing.</p><p>If the remaining companies do not fit your offer, a second phone number will not fix the targeting. Change the list brief.</p></div><aside><h3>Buy what your list is missing.</h3><p>That might mean a current contact at a good account, mobiles for a field-based role, or more companies in a segment producing useful conversations.</p><p>Start with a small batch you can inspect and work. Set its size around your calling capacity and follow-up load.</p></aside></div>
    <p class="lp-small">Choosing how to work the queue? Use our <a href="/resources/best-cold-calling-dialers/">cold calling dialler comparison</a> to match single-line or multi-line calling to your list and budget.</p>
    <div class="lb-measure"><strong>Measure the route properly.</strong><p>Track unique contacts attempted, intended-buyer conversations, useful referrals and held meetings. Compare similar accounts and roles using a similar attempt policy. Mark contacts reached through more than one route so one person does not become two results.</p><a href="/resources/b2b-lead-providers/#cost-check">Calculate the cost of your usable contacts →</a></div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="lp-section" id="calling-template"><div class="lp-wrap">
    <div class="lp-heading"><p class="lp-kicker">05 / Free. No email required.</p><h2>Build a calling list<br>your team can work.</h2><p>Get the buying brief right, then keep the information that makes the next call easier.</p></div>
    <ol class="lb-prep-steps"><li><h3>Define the company and the buyer.</h3><p>Write down the territory, company fit, relevant site and buying role. Include exclusions. A senior title alone does not establish who owns the decision.</p></li><li><h3>Ask for the fields you need.</h3><p>Request mobile, work direct and company numbers separately, with source and check date. Ask what happens when the required field is missing and what verification actually covered.</p></li><li><h3>Check a small sample.</h3><p>Review the role, location, number type, duplicate people and existing CRM matches. Flag shared phones. Keep the original records so you can trace a correction.</p></li><li><h3>Test the import and the next action.</h3><p>Map fields into your CRM using a few records first. Check phone formatting, country codes, extensions and IDs. Keep do-not-call records out of the calling queue and scheduled callbacks out of the unworked list.</p></li></ol>
    <div class="lb-download"><div><p class="lp-kicker">Calling-list template</p><h3>One contact per row.<br>Every route kept separate.</h3><p>A blank CSV with account and contact IDs, role, location, three number fields, source, outcome and next action.</p><a class="lp-button" href="/assets/downloads/cold-calling-list-template.csv" download="CallTeam-Cold-Calling-List-Template.csv">Download the free CSV <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><p class="lp-small">Open in Excel or Google Sheets. Import phone and ID columns as text. Map fields to your CRM before uploading. No sample contacts or automatic number verification are included.</p></div><div class="lb-template-guide"><h4>Keep these distinctions</h4><dl><dt>Number source and check date</dt><dd>Record where each number came from. Check dates use YYYY-MM-DD.</dd><dt>Last route and outcome</dt><dd>Separate no answer, voicemail, screened, wrong number, referral, buyer conversation and stop request.</dd><dt>Next action and due date</dt><dd>Record what happens next, with the prospect's time zone. A callback belongs on its agreed schedule.</dd><dt>Shared number flag</dt><dd>Coordinate at company level. Different people can share a real switchboard number.</dd></dl></div></div>
    <details class="lb-field-guide"><summary>What is in the CSV?</summary><p>The 24 columns cover: company, website, account ID, contact ID, contact name, job title, location, time zone, account fit, mobile, work direct, company phone, number sources, number check dates, first route, last route, last outcome, last attempt, next action, next action due, shared number flag, do-not-call status, owner and notes.</p><p>Keep extensions in the work direct field until mapped to your CRM. Label the source and check date for each route in the source/date fields. Use notes for an unknown number type instead of silently calling it mobile. Empty fields mean missing information, not a completed check.</p></details>
    <div class="lp-note"><strong>Choosing where to buy the data?</strong><p>Use our <a href="/resources/b2b-lead-providers/">10 best B2B lead generation databases comparison</a> for provider strengths, drawbacks and a supplier test scorecard. For the list brief itself, see <a href="/articles/b2b-prospect-list-building-services/">what to ask a list-building service to deliver</a>.</p></div>
  </div></section>

